  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 11 — agent friendly */}
  <section id="agents" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
    <h2 id="agents-title">Built for developers — and their coding agents</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">The repository is written to be read by a machine as well as a person. Claude, Codex, Cursor and Antigravity all pick up the same files; none of this implies any partnership with them.</p>
    <div class="tri">
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>Rules that are written down</h3><p><code>AGENTS.md</code> carries 18 architectural rules, and <code>skills/</code> carries 16 task guides, so an agent loads the one matching its job instead of reading the whole repository.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>A graph small enough to hold</h3><p>One production dependency and a flat package layout. Comments explain <em>why</em>, because the code already says what.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>A suite that catches the agent</h3><p><b>1,036 tests.</b> This is what makes agent-assisted change safe rather than merely fast: a wrong edit fails here, not in a customer's checkout.</p></div>
    </div>
    <ol class="agentflow">
      <li>Clone</li><li>Read <code>AGENTS.md</code></li><li>Modify a module</li><li><code>docker compose up</code></li><li>Tests catch it</li><li>Deploy</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="fineprint">Separately, the MCP module exposes a running store over the Model Context Protocol. Every tool calls the same domain service a REST request would — an agent never touches the database — and <code>ReadOnly</code> withholds every mutating tool while you are deciding how far to trust it.</p>
  </section>

  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 12 — partners */}
  <section id="partners" class="section partners-section" aria-labelledby="partners-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="partners-title">Partners and integrations</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">One partner, and twenty-six integrations. The distinction matters: a partner hosts this site, an integration is a module in the repository that speaks to that provider's API. None of the providers below endorses either project.</p>
      <div class="partner-row">
        <a class="partner-card" href="https://www.cloudflare.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <img src="/assets/cloudflare.svg" width="32" height="32" alt="" loading="lazy">
          <span><b>Cloudflare</b><span>Hosting partner — serves this site from Workers</span></span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="integrations">
        {[['Payments', pay, 9], ['Shipping', ship, 11], ['Messaging and search', msg, 6]].map(([title, items, n]) => (
          <div class="int-group">
            <h3>{title} <span>{n} modules</span></h3>
            <ul>
              {(items as string[][]).map(([name, mark]) => (
                <li>{mark && <img src={`/assets/brands/${mark}.svg`} width="16" height="16" alt="" loading="lazy">}{name}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">Every name above is a directory under <code>ext/</code> in the GoCommerce repository. Install one by importing it; leave it out and none of its code is in your binary. <a href="/gocommerce/#modules">All 44 modules</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 13 — open source */}
  <section id="ownership" class="section ownership-section" aria-labelledby="own-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="own-title">Open source. Self-hosted. No platform fees.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">Run the code on your own infrastructure. No mandatory subscription and no fee for using the software. Your servers and any third-party services still cost what they cost — the software is free, hosting is not.</p>
      <ul class="ownwall">
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-source"/></svg>MIT licensed, both projects</li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-server"/></svg>Your infrastructure</li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-db"/></svg>Your database</li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-git"/></svg>Fork and rebrand freely</li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Nothing phones home</li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-coin"/></svg>No hosted tier to graduate to</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fineprint">Looking for a self-hosted, open-source alternative to a hosted ecommerce platform? GoCommerce and Svelte Commerce give you the backend, the storefront and the infrastructure. They do not reproduce every feature of a mature commercial platform, and this site will not pretend otherwise — read the <a href="/gocommerce/#roadmap">roadmap</a> before you commit.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

